          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The most commonly accepted standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundation+of+mathematics"} foundation of mathematics
            today is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            commonly known as
            %em
              Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZFC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
            for short. There are many variations on that theory (including
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            and
            = succeed "-based" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
          %p
            Accompanying ZFC, especially taking into account the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+foundation"} axiom of foundation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cumulative+hierarchy"} cumulative hierarchy
            organized by an
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+number"} ordinal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
            and building to higher ranks by applying a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
            operation to get to a next successor ordinal rank, and taking
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unions"} unions
            to get to limit ordinal ranks. This iterative conception finds alternative expression in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+set+theory"} algebraic set theory
          %h2#history History
          %p
            The first version was developed by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+Zermelo"} Ernst Zermelo
            in 1908; in 1922,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Abraham+Fraenkel"} Abraham Fraenkel
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thoralf+Skolem"} Thoralf Skolem
            independently proposed a precise
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+logic"} first-order
            version with the
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+replacement"} axiom of replacement
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+foundation"} axiom of foundation
            in 1925. All of these versions included the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZF
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZF
            if it is taken out.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZFC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
            is similar to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
            theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NBG"} NBG
            (due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+von+Neumann"} John von Neumann
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Bernays"} Paul Bernays
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kurt+G%C3%B6del"} Kurt Gödel
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morse%E2%80%93Kelley+set+theory"} MK
            (due to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anthony+Morse"} Anthony Morse
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Kelley"} John Kelley
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ZFC
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
          %p
            Contemporary set theorists often accept additional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+cardinal"} large cardinal
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ZFC
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi MK
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} MK
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+determinacy"} axiom of determinacy
            (or various weaker versions of it) or the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+constructibility"} axiom of constructibility
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ZFC
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative
            mathematics. Then there are alternatives on a different basis, notably
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/New+Foundations"} NFU
            (a very
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} impredicative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            with a set of all sets) and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            (a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
          %p
            (The source for this history, especially the dates, is mostly
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zermelo%E2%80%93Fraenkel_set_theory"} the English Wikipedia
          %h2#axioms Axioms
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZFC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+type+theory"} simply typed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+theory"} first-order
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            called the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cumulative+hierarchy"} cumulative hierarchy
            and a binary
            %strong membership
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate"} predicate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∈
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            in standard
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZFC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+set"} pure set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            is said to belong to, be in, be a
            %strong member
            of, or be an
            %strong element
            of a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            is said to have the member
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
          %p
            There are also variations of ZFC with non-set terms such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/urelements"} urelements
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classes"} classes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
            also has no elements); sets are usually those classes that are themselves elements (members) of sets. Urelements are also called
            = succeed "," do
              %em atoms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZF
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZF
            with atoms included is sometimes called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFA"} ZFA
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ZFU
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFU
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+extensionality"}> Extensionality
                \: If two sets have the same members, then they are
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equal
                and themselves members of the same sets. See
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+extensionality"} axiom of extensionality
                for variations, such as whether this is taken as a definition or an axiomatisation of equality of sets, and how the condition might be strengthened if (10) is left out.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+the+null+set"}> Null Set
                \: There is an
                = succeed ":" do
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ∅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \empty
                with no elements. By (1), it follows that this set is unique; by even the weakest version of (5), it is enough to state the existence of some set. (Analogous remarks apply to axioms (3), (4), (6), (7), and (8), except when (5) is omitted and (4) and (6) are needed to derive it.)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+pairing"}> Pairing
                \: If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
                are sets, then there is a set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{a,b\}
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unordered+pairing"} unordered pairing
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                or
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{a\} = \{a,a\}
                by (1). The higher finitary versions rely also on (4) (with
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                        %mo ,
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo ∪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi b
                        %mo ,
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{a,b,c\} = \{a\} \cup \{b,c\}
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+scheme"} axiom scheme
                if (4) is omitted (but nobody seems to do that).
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+union"}> Union
                \: If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                is a set, then there is a set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcup \mathcal{C}
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/union"} union
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∪
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cup B
                for
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcup \{A,B\}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∪
                      %mi B
                      %mo ∪
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cup B \cup C
                for
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                        %mo ,
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcup \{A,B,C\}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo =
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcup \{A\} = A
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                      %mi ∅
                      %mo =
                      %mi ∅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigcup \empty = \empty
                (using (1) to prove these results), so no special notation is needed for these.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+separation"}> Separation/Specification/Comprehension
                \: Given any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate"} predicate
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi[x]
                in the language of set theory with the chosen
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+variable"} free variable
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                of type
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                is a set, then there is a set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi U
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x \in U \;|\; \phi[x]\}
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                given by
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi[x]
                holds. There are many variations, from Bounded Separation to Full Comprehension, which we should probably describe at
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+separation"} axiom of separation
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+scheme"} axiom scheme
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi NBG
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} NBG
                (but not completely in
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi MK
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} MK
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+replacement"}> Replacement/Collection
                \: Given a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate"} predicate
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi[x,Y]
                with the chosen
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+variables"} free variables
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                of type
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                is a set and if for every
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                there is a unique
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi[x,Y]
                holds, then there is set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi ι
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi Y
                        %mo .
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi ψ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\iota\,Y.\;\psi[x,Y] \;|\; x \in U\}
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                under
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ψ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                such that there is an element
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi[x,Y]
                holds; if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ψ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi[x]
                is a defined term, then we write
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi Ψ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\Psi[x] \;|\; x \in U\}
                for
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi ι
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi Y
                        %mo .
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi Y
                        %mo =
                        %mi Ψ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\iota\,Y.\; Y = \Psi[x] \;|\; x \in U\}
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+replacement"} axiom of replacement
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+scheme"} axiom scheme
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi NBG
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} NBG
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi MK
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} MK
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi ι
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi Y
                        %mo .
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi ψ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi U
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\iota\,Y.\;\psi[x,Y] \;|\; x \in U \;|\; \phi[x]\}
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+power+sets"}> Power Sets
                \: If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                is a set, then there is a set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒫
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}U
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                whose elements are all elements of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset+collection"} Subset Collection
                or (even weaker)
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+sets"} Exponentiation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+infinity"}> Infinity
                \: There is a set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
                of
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+number"} finite ordinals
                as pure sets. Normally one states that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ∅
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \empty \in \omega
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \cup \{a\} \in \omega
                whenever
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ω
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in \omega
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peano+arithmetic"} Peano's axioms
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind-infinite+set"} Dedekind-infinite set
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"}> Choice
                \: If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                is a set of pairwise disjoint sets, each of which has an element, then there is a set with exactly one element from each element of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
                %em not
                unique, nor can we construct a canonical version which is, so we do not give it any name or notation. This version is the simplest to state in the language of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ZFC
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
                for further discussion and weak versions. It is possible to incorporate (9) into (5) or (6), but this seems to be rare.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+foundation"}> Foundation/Regularity/Induction
                \: Given a formula
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
                with a chosen
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+variable"} free variable
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                of type
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
                holds whenever
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi[a/X]
                holds for every
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
                holds absolutely. For variations (including the axiom of anti-foundation), see
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+foundation"} axiom of foundation
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+scheme"} axiom scheme
                can be made into a single axiom even in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ZFC
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
                itself (although not in versions with intuitionistic logic; in that case it can be made a single axiom only in a class theory).
          %h2#variations Variations
          %p
            Zermelo's original version consists of axioms (1–5) and (7–9), in a somewhat imprecise form (which affects the interpretation of 5) of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher-order+logic"} higher-order
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
          %p
            The modern
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZF
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZF
            consists of (1–8) and (10), using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZFC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
            adds (9) and is thus the strongest version without classes or additional axioms. The version originally formulated by Fraenkel and Skolem did not include (10), although the three founders all eventually accepted it.
          %p
            It is common to take
            %strong Zermelo set theory
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Z}
            ) to be
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZF
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZF
            without (6), although Zermelo never accepted the first-order formulation, and his axioms did not include the Axiom of Foundation (10). Note that the weakest form (Weak Replacement) of (6) follows from (7) and (5), so it holds even in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Z}
          %p
            Another variant is
            %strong bounded Zermelo set theory
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi BZ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BZ
            ), which is like
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Z}
            but with only Bounded Separation; this is of interest to category theorists because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi BZC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BZC
            is equivalent to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
          %h3#constructive_versions Constructive versions
          %p
            See also
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+set+theory"} constructive set theory
          %p
            The most well-known foundations for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            through material set theory are
            = succeed "'s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Aczel"} Peter Aczel
            %strong constructive Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CZF
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CZF
            ) and
            = succeed "'s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Myhill"} John Myhill
            %strong intuitionistic Zermelo–Fraenkel set theory
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi IZF
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} IZF
            ).
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CZF
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CZF
            uses axioms (1–8) and (10), usually weak forms, in
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi IZF
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} IZF
            is simliar, but it uses Full Separation for (5) and the full strength of (7); Myhill's original version uses only Replacement for (6), but Collection (equivalent to Strong Collection using Full Separation) is standard now.
          %p
            Note that adding (9) to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi IZF
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} IZF
            implies
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            and so makes
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ZFC
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+choice"} dependent choice
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/COSHEP"} COSHEP
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
            's survey of material and structural set theories (
            %a{:href => "#Shulman2018"}> Shulman 2018
            ) takes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi CPZ
                    %mrow
                      %mo ↺
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CPZ^{\circlearrowleft-}
            as the most basic form; it consists of (1–4) and the weakest versions (Bounded Separation and Weak Replacement) of (5&amp;6) in intuitionistic logic. Adding (10) gives
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi CPZ
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CPZ^{-}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi CPZ
                      %mo ↺
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CPZ^{\circlearrowleft}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CPZ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CPZ
            = succeed "." do
              %strong constructive pre-Zermelo set theory
          %p
            Myhill has another version,
            %strong constructive set theory
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CST
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CST
            ); this consists of (1–4), Bounded Separation for (5), Replacement for (6), the weakest (Exponentiation) form of (7), (8), and a weak version (Dependent Choice) of (9). It also uses a variation of the language, with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/urelements"} urelements
            for natural numbers; note that the existence of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            still follows using (6). This classifies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CST
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CST
            as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} C
                  %mi Π
                  %msup
                    %mi ZF
                    %mo ↺
                  %mo +
                  %mi DC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{C}{\Pi}ZF^{\circlearrowleft} + DC
            in Shulman's system if one ignores the use of urelements and strengthens Replacement to Strong Collection.
          %h3#class_theories Class theories
          %p
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morse--Kelley+class+theory"} Morse–Kelley class theory
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi MK
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} MK
            ) features both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+classes"} proper classes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            can include
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantification"} quantification
            over classes; the same holds in (6) and (10), although this does not add any additional strength.
          %p
            %strong Von Neumann–Bernays–Gödel class theory
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi NBG
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} NBG
            ) uses the same language as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi MK
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} MK
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZFC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
            and also allows for a finite axiomatisation; we replace the formulas in (5) and (6) with classes, and add some special cases of (5) for subclasses, one for each logical connective. (It is provable that plain
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ZF
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZF
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi NBG
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} NBG
            escapes this conclusion by extending the language with the notion of classes.)
          %p
            One can also rework all of the weak versions of set theory above into a class theory like
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi NBG
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} NBG
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi MK
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} MK
          %h3#large_cardinals Large cardinals
          %p
            One often adds axioms for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+cardinals"} large cardinals
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ZFC
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℵ
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \aleph_0
            exists. These additional axioms are most commonly studied in the context of a material set theory, but they work just as well in a structural set theory.
          %p
            Note that adding an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inaccessible+cardinal"} inaccessible cardinal
            (commonly considered the smallest sort of large cardinal) to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZFC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
            is already stronger than
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi MK
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} MK
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi MK
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} MK
            as the sets in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\kappa
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mrow
                        %mi κ
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_{\kappa+1}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi MK
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} MK
            to get something even stronger.
          %p
            It is often convenient to assume that one always has more large cardinals when necessary. You cannot say this in an absolute sense, but you can adopt the axiom that every set belongs to some
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+universe"} Grothendieck universe
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZFC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
            makes
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tarski-Grothendieck+set+theory"} Tarski-Grothendieck set theory
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi TG
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TG
            ). This is not the last word, however; you can make it stronger by adding classes in the style of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi MK
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} MK
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi TG
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TG
          %h3#miscellaneous_variations Miscellaneous variations
          %p
            The
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+constructibility"} axiom of constructibility
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo =
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = L
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ZF
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZF
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructible+universe"} constructible universe
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo =
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = L
            implies the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            and thus also
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            even with intuitionistic logic.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo =
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = L
            also implies the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+continuum+hypothesis"} generalized continuum hypothesis
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi GCH
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GCH
            ), which is how Gödel originally proved that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi GCH
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GCH
            was consistent with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ZFC
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+cardinal"} measurable cardinal
            implies that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ≠
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \neq L
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo =
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = L
            as potentially “true.”
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+determinacy"} axiom of determinacy
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi AD
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AD
            ) is another axiom that can be added to
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ZF
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZF
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/game"} game
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi AD
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AD
            is inconsistent with the full axiom of choice, although it is consistent with dependent choice. A weaker form of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi AD
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AD
            called “projective determinacy” is consistent with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi AC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AC
            and is equiconsistent with certain large cardinal assertions.
          %p
            The
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi GCH
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GCH
            itself, or its negation, could also be regarded as an additional axiom that can be added to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ZF
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZF
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi GCH
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GCH
            or its negation. The equiconsistency of projective determinacy with a large cardinal assertion can be regarded as a step in this direction.
          %h3#relation_to_structural_set_theories Relation to structural set theories
          %p
            The structural set theory
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            is equivalent to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi BZC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BZC
            in that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
            in that theory satisfies
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ETCS
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ETCS
            while the well-founded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pure+sets"} pure sets
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ETCS
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ETCS
            satisfy
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi BZC
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} BZC
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
            's
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SEAR"} SEARC
            is equivalent to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZFC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
            in the same way.
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SEAR
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SEAR
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ZF
                      %mo ↺
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZF^{\circlearrowleft}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZF
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZF
            without (10), in a weaker sense of equivalence.
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Shulman2018"} Shulman 2018
            is an extensive survey of variations of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ZFC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
            and variations of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ETCS
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ETCS
            (mostly weak ones), showing how these correspond.
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+set+theory"} algebraic set theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cumulative+hierarchy"} cumulative hierarchy
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+ZFC"} structural ZFC
          %h2#references References
          %p Zermelo’s axiomatisation grew out of his reflections on his proofs of the well-ordering theorem (1904/08) and was published in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+Zermelo"}> Ernst Zermelo
              ,
              %em Untersuchungen über die Grundlagen der Mengenlehre I
              , Math. Ann.
              %strong 65
              (1908) pp.261-81. (
              %a{:href => "http://gdz.sub.uni-goettingen.de/dms/load/img/?PPN=GDZPPN002262002"}> gdz
              )
          %p English versions of the early key texts on set theory by Zermelo, Fraenkel, Skolem, von Neumann et al. can be found in
          %ul
            %li
              J. van Heijenoort,
              %em From Frege to Gödel - A Sourcebook in Mathematical Logic 1879-1931
              , Harvard UP 1967.
          %p
            There are many texts which discuss ZFC and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cumulative+hierarchy"} cumulative hierarchy
            from a traditional (material) set-theoretic perspective. A good example is
          %ul
            %li
              Kenneth Kunen,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Set Theory: An Introduction to Independence Proofs
          %p
            A classification of axioms of variants of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ZFC
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ZFC
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theories"} structural set theories
          %ul#Shulman2018
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Shulman"} Michael Shulman
              (2018). Comparing material and structural set theories.
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.05204"} arXiv:1808.05204
          %p
            For a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+function"} polynomial function
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+measure"} Lesbegue measurability
            is independent of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
          %ul
